# Use Installed Plugins & Dependencies

## Core Principle: Leverage Existing Tools

**Always prefer installed plugins, dependencies, and established patterns over custom implementations.**

## Plugin-First Development Approach

### 1. Check Available Dependencies First
Before writing custom code, verify if functionality exists in:
- **Package.json dependencies** - Check [package.json](mdc:package.json) for available packages
- **Built-in Node.js modules** - Use standard library when possible
- **Express.js middleware** - Leverage existing middleware patterns
- **React ecosystem** - Use established React patterns and hooks

### 2. Database Operations
```javascript
// ✅ USE installed database utilities
import { executeQuery, executeCrudOperation } from '../config/database.js';
import optimizedQueryService from '../services/optimizedQueryService.js';

// ❌ AVOID custom database wrappers
const customDbWrapper = new CustomDatabaseWrapper();
```

### 3. Authentication & Security
```javascript
// ✅ USE established middleware
import { authenticateCookie, authorize as authorizeCookie } from '../middleware/cookieAuth.js';
import { adminMiddleware } from '../routes/optimizedRoutes.js';

// ❌ AVOID custom auth implementations
const customAuth = new CustomAuthService();
```

### 4. State Management
```javascript
// ✅ USE TanStack Query (already installed)
import { useQuery, useMutation } from '@tanstack/react-query';

// ❌ AVOID custom state management
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(customReducer, initialState);
```

### 5. Real-time Features
```javascript
// ✅ USE Socket.io (already installed)
import { io } from 'socket.io-client';
import websocketService from '../services/websocketService.js';

// ❌ AVOID custom WebSocket implementations
const customWebSocket = new WebSocket('ws://localhost');
```

